WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.980
What makes an operating system

2
00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:04.700
real time? Correctness depends on

3
00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.480
producing the right result within

4
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.620
its timing requirement. A low

5
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.520
average response time alone cannot

6
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.940
establish that every important deadline

7
00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:13.620
will be met.

8
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.460
A hard real-time requirement treats

9
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.420
a missed deadline as unacceptable

10
00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.520
for the specified task. A

11
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.600
soft requirement allows degraded value

12
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:23.880
or service when a result

13
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.500
arrives late. Apply the distinction

14
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.240
to the stated application instead

15
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.480
of assuming that any fast

16
00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:30.780
computer is real time.

17
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.100
The course job notation contains

18
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.860
release R, execution demand C,

19
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.460
and relative deadline D. Its

20
00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.420
absolute deadline is R plus

21
00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.980
D. A release says when

22
00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:43.700
the job becomes eligible, while

23
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.580
execution demand says how much

24
00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.060
processor service it needs.

25
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.200
For an authored job released

26
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.080
at time five with execution

27
00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.240
demand two and relative deadline

28
00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.780
seven, the absolute deadline is

29
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.460
twelve. If it starts at

30
00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.340
nine and runs continuously for

31
00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.280
two units, it finishes at

32
00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.100
eleven. It has waited four

33
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.780
units and still meets its

34
00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:05.180
deadline.

35
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.700
For a periodic task, the

36
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.840
first parameter is period T

37
00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:11.840
rather than one release time.

38
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.320
Instances arrive at zero, T,

39
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.040
twice T, and so on,

40
00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.200
each carrying its own execution

41
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.620
demand and absolute deadline. A

42
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.080
fresh release does not erase

43
00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.820
an unfinished previous instance.

44
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.180
Consider a task with period

45
00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.960
ten, execution demand six, and

46
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.320
relative deadline ten. Its first

47
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.740
instance is released at zero

48
00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.900
with deadline ten, then another

49
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.760
arrives at ten with deadline

50
00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.200
twenty. Keep the instance identities

51
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.400
separate when drawing preemption and

52
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.420
remaining execution.

53
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.320
Rate monotonic uses a fixed

54
00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.720
task priority determined by period:

55
00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.460
shorter period means higher priority.

56
00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.440
Deadline monotonic uses shorter relative

57
00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.100
deadline instead. They coincide only

58
00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.580
when the relevant ordering keys

59
00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:58.900
happen to produce the same

60
00:01:58.900 --> 00:01:59.260
order.

61
00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.320
Earliest deadline first compares the

62
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:04.280
absolute deadlines of ready job

63
00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:06.740
instances. The ranking can change

64
00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:08.800
as instances arrive, so it

65
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.460
is a dynamic-priority policy. It

66
00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.100
does not mean shortest execution

67
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.360
demand or smallest relative deadline

68
00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.980
considered without release time.

69
00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.400
Mark releases, completion times and

70
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.160
absolute deadlines before calculating any

71
00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.440
average. A job still requiring

72
00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.460
execution when its deadline arrives

73
00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:29.920
has missed it. Finishing exactly

74
00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.300
at a deadline counts as

75
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.400
meeting it in the interval

76
00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.380
model used here.

77
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.240
Things to take note of.

78
00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.860
Separate release, execution demand, period

79
00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:41.280
and relative deadline, then compute

80
00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.140
each instance absolute deadline. Use

81
00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:46.460
the scheduling policy actual comparison

82
00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:48.180
key and test the deadline

83
00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:49.880
of each job, not just

84
00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.300
the average completion time.
